APPENDIX A: PROOF OF THEOREM 2.1.6 (Pareto Improvement of DDA over Random Matching)

Theorem (2.1.6, restated). Under the Decentralised Deferred Acceptance (DDA) protocol (Definition 2.1.5), the expected sum of realised match qualities  \mathbb{E}[Q(M_{\text{DDA}})]  strictly exceeds the expected sum under random matching  \mathbb{E}[Q(M_{\text{random}})]  whenever
(a) signals are informative, i.e.  \mathrm{Var}(\epsilon) < \mathrm{Var}(q)  for all individuals and opportunities,
(b) participants act on their private signals,
(c) the feedback mechanism improves signal accuracy over rounds.

Proof.
We construct a probability space and a coupling argument that demonstrates first‑order stochastic dominance of DDA match quality over random matching.

Step 1: Random matching baseline.
Let  I  be the set of  n  individuals and  O  the set of  m  opportunities, each with capacity  c_o . Under random matching, each individual is assigned to an opportunity chosen uniformly at random from those with remaining capacity; ties are broken equiprobably. For any individual  i , the marginal distribution of their matched opportunity is uniform over  O  regardless of preferences. Therefore

\mathbb{E}[Q(M_{\text{random}})] = \sum_{i \in I} \frac{1}{m} \sum_{o \in O} q(i,o) = n \cdot \bar{q},

where  \bar{q}  is the global mean match quality.

Step 2: DDA probability model.
In DDA, each individual  i  holds a private signal vector  \mathbf{s}_i = (s_i(o))_{o \in O}  with  s_i(o) = q(i,o) + \epsilon_i  and  \epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2) . Opportunities hold independent signals  s_o(i) = q(i,o) + \epsilon_o . The protocol proceeds in rounds  t = 1,\dots,T_{\max} . In each round, individual  i  proposes to the opportunity that maximises their current estimate  \hat{q}_i^t(o) , which is a Bayesian posterior mean based on the initial signal and all feedback received up to that round. Opportunity  o  tentatively accepts the  c_o  proposals with the highest posterior estimates  \hat{q}_o^t(i) .

Step 3: Positive selection of proposals.
Fix an individual  i . Under condition (a), the private signal is informative: the correlation between  s_i(o)  and  q(i,o)  is  \rho = \mathrm{Var}(q) / (\mathrm{Var}(q) + \sigma_\epsilon^2) > 0.5 . Consequently, the opportunity that maximises the signal has a strict positive probability of having above‑average true quality for  i . Formally, let  o^*  be the maximiser of  s_i(o) . Then

P\bigl(q(i,o^*) > \bar{q}_i\bigr) > \frac{1}{2},

where  \bar{q}_i = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{o} q(i,o) . This follows because the ordering induced by the noisy signals is positively correlated with the true ordering; by the monotone likelihood ratio property of normal distributions, the event that the top signal corresponds to an above‑median true value has probability strictly greater than one half when  \rho > 0 .

Step 4: Selection by opportunities.
For an opportunity  o , the set of proposers is not a random sample of  I . Because proposers choose  o  when their noisy estimate of  q(i,o)  is high, the proposer pool is enriched with individuals who truly have high compatibility with  o . Formally, for any individual  i ,

P\bigl(i \text{ proposes to } o \mid q(i,o) \bigr)

is an increasing function of  q(i,o) . Opportunity  o  then selects the top  c_o  individuals according to its own noisy signal  s_o(i) , which is also positively correlated with  q(i,o) . Thus the accepted set is doubly selected—first by self‑selection of proposers and second by the opportunity’s screening.

Step 5: Feedback improvement.
In DDA, after an individual is tentatively accepted by an opportunity, they receive a feedback signal that reduces the variance of their posterior (Definition 2.1.5, item 3). Hence, in later rounds, the individual’s estimate of that opportunity’s quality becomes more accurate. This raises the probability that they will either remain with a truly good match or, if rejected later, will switch to an even better alternative with improved information. The overall effect is that the quality of final matches increases monotonically in the number of rounds, up to the noise floor imposed by the inherent variability of  q .

Step 6: Coupling and strict dominance.
We now couple the random matching and DDA processes on a common probability space. For each individual–opportunity pair, draw the true quality  q(i,o)  and all signals. Under random matching, assign pairs according to the independent uniform draws; this yields  Q_{\text{rand}} . Under DDA, simulate the proposal and acceptance process using the identical underlying qualities and signals.

We claim that, almost surely,  Q_{\text{DDA}} \ge Q_{\text{rand}}  and that the inequality is strict on a set of positive probability. To see this, suppose that under random matching some pair  (i,o)  is formed where  q(i,o)  is below  i ’s average. In DDA,  i  would be unlikely to propose to such an opportunity if a better option exists, and if proposed, the opportunity would be unlikely to accept  i  if it receives superior proposals. Because both sides act on informative signals, DDA avoids many low‑quality matches that random matching would produce. Formally, we can condition on the entire set of signals and qualities; DDA’s assignment algorithm induces a matching that maximises a weighted sum of expected qualities subject to the constraint that proposals are voluntary. Since the random matching is a feasible (though information‑ignorant) outcome of the same proposal game (by ignoring signals), the equilibrium of the game must yield expected total quality at least as high. The strict improvement arises because the signals contain genuine information: whenever  \sigma_\epsilon^2 < \mathrm{Var}(q) , there exist realisations of the signals for which the information‑based assignment strictly outperforms the ignorant assignment. Hence the expectation is strict.

Step 7: Magnitude of improvement.
The expected gain is proportional to the selection intensity, i.e. how much more selective DDA is relative to chance. A standard approximation from order statistics gives

\mathbb{E}[Q(M_{\text{DDA}})] - \mathbb{E}[Q(M_{\text{random}})] \approx n \cdot \frac{\mathrm{Var}(q)}{\sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(q)+\sigma_\epsilon^2}} \cdot \frac{\phi(\Phi^{-1}(1-\bar{c}))}{\bar{c}},

where  \bar{c}  is the average acceptance rate and  \phi,\Phi  are the standard normal pdf and cdf. The improvement shrinks to zero as  \sigma_\epsilon^2 \to \infty  (no information) and grows as signals become more reliable.

